\title{plantstressR: Multivariate Phenotyping of Plant Stress Response Signatures}
\description{
\code{plantstressR} answers a single question: \emph{what is the physiological stress
signature of a plant, and how do its traits interact?} The workflow is
deliberately short and always starts from one tidy table of physiological
traits measured in a control treatment and in one or more stress treatments.
\enumerate{
\item \code{\link[=validate_stress_data]{validate_stress_data()}} checks the design (control level, replication,
numeric traits, missing values) before anything is computed.
\item \code{\link[=calculate_sri]{calculate_sri()}} converts every trait into a signed, standardized
Stress Response Index (SRI) relative to the control treatment.
\item \code{\link[=integrated_stress_index]{integrated_stress_index()}} collapses the per-trait indices into one
weighted integrated stress index (ISI) used to rank genotypes or
treatments.
\item \code{\link[=plot_stress_signature]{plot_stress_signature()}} draws the signature as a heat map or a radar
plot.
\item \code{\link[=stress_network]{stress_network()}} estimates a regularized partial-correlation network
among traits and detects the modules that respond to stress together, and
\code{\link[=stress_module_scores]{stress_module_scores()}} scores those modules with the trait indices.
\item \code{\link[=stress_ordination]{stress_ordination()}} places samples in a multivariate trait space when a
principal component view is required.
}

\code{\link[=stress_tolerance_index]{stress_tolerance_index()}} sits beside this workflow: it computes the
classical productivity-based selection indices (\code{STI}, \code{SSI}, \code{GMP}, \code{TOL}
and others) from a single yield trait, so that a physiological ranking can be
checked against the agronomic one.
}
